#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Regenerate the clips and stills on the engine's examples page.
#
# Sibling of ./refresh-screenshots, which does the same job for the games. The
# two are kept apart because the games are each their own repo and stage their
# own shot through a --screenshot argument, while the examples are a dozen
# binaries in one repo that take no arguments of their own. What stages them is
# this file: a line per example saying how long to wait, how long to film,
# which second the still comes from, and what to press while it runs.
#
# That is the whole reason it exists. The examples page had stills from the
# 28th of September on it on the 15th of October, and nothing anywhere could
# take them again: they had been shot by hand, once each.
#
# **A still of a physics engine is the wrong picture.** Every example here
# does something, and a dozen of them were being shown off by a photograph of
# the moment after they had finished doing it. So each one is a short silent
# clip that loops, with a still pulled out of the middle of it to stand in
# until the clip loads and to be the picture a readme can use.
#
# **It renders rather than films.** Until spec 0084 this ran a real window and
# filmed the whole screen with ffmpeg against avfoundation, cropping the
# window out of the result, and pressed its keys with CGEvent at whatever was
# frontmost. Five things came with that and every one of them cost something.
#
# It needed a Screen Recording grant. It was macOS only, twice over. The
# window had to stay in front, so a run was something you had to leave the
# machine alone for, and a run that lost the front filmed the wrong thing
# rather than failing. The display had to stay awake, and four takes across
# two days went to it sleeping. And it never came back twice the same, so
# nothing could tell a clip that had changed from a clip that had been
# refilmed.
#
# The engine draws the frames now: `--film-for <seconds>` writes every frame
# it draws to stdout as raw pixels, on fixed steps with no clock in it, and
# ffmpeg reads them off the pipe. Nothing lands on the disc, no window opens,
# the keys go to the game and nowhere else, and two runs give the same bytes.
#
# The keys are a file the engine plays rather than events posted at the
# screen: the table below is compiled into the log format of spec 0080, which
# is the format `--record` writes. Spec 0081 built that so a staged run could
# be recorded by hand instead of written out; this is the other end of it.
set -euo pipefail
cd "$(dirname "$0")"

# How good a clip is, as x264 counts it. 22 is a quiet number for flat shaded
# geometry on black.
#
# The sixteen come to 29 MB at it, against 25 before this rendered them. A
# drawn frame is sharper than a screen capture taken at two pixels to a point
# and scaled down to one, and the scaling was doing some of x264's work for
# it. The line here used to say the page came in under two megabytes, which
# had not been true of the committed clips for a long time.
QUALITY=${QUALITY:-22}
# What the page plays at. The engine draws at its own fixed step, which is a
# sixtieth; ffmpeg drops to this on the way out.
RATE=${RATE:-30}

# What to film, as: example, wait seconds, film seconds, poster seconds, keys.
#
# tower is not here, and that is a decision rather than an oversight. It went
# on 2026-10-16 because cairn is the better example of the same thing: four
# blocks to a level against two, grained wood against one flat colour, and a
# tower that comes apart because taking it apart is the game. It came off the
# page, and then out of the engine altogether. See blitzkit spec 0054.
#
# The wait is how long into the clip the keys start. There is no camera to
# open any more, so a clip begins on the example's own first frame and the
# wait is time the game runs with nothing pressed.
#
# The keys are what somebody would press while watching. A key is a letter,
# `space`, `enter`, or an arrow as `left`/`right`/`up`/`down`, which are the
# names `KeyboardKey::named` already knows, so nothing here translates them;
# `hold:<key>:<seconds>` holds one down, and the key may be several joined by
# a plus, as `hold:w+left:0.8`, which holds them together and is how a corner
# is walked rather than pivoted on; `pin:` is the same thing, kept apart
# because a pinned walk is meant to end against a wall;
# `tap:<key>:<many>:<gap>` hits it over and over;
# `shove:<across>:<down>:<held>` presses the mouse at that fraction of the
# frame for that long; `zoom:<ticks>` turns the wheel over the middle of it;
# and a number on its own is a wait in seconds.
#
# **The timings mean what they say now.** Each key used to cost about three
# tenths of a second of fronting the window and sleeping either side of the
# press, which the table's numbers were written around. A key is an instant in
# the log, so the gaps between them are the gaps written here.
#
# **The arcade's route is places rather than keys, per spec 0049.** The column
# says `film:clip` and the route itself is `tour::clip`, which reads the
# building's own accessors for every place it names: the pond, the planets,
# the bookcase, the baths, the pool. Written out here it would be a second
# account of where all of those are, and this file is the last place that
# should hold one.
#
# 46 seconds for a route that is 90 metres of walking and 14 of standing.
# Run in a test rather than measured off a take, by
# `the_clip_walks_the_whole_route`, which drives the game's own frame loop and
# prints what the route costs: 42.1 seconds. The rest is a few seconds of the
# pool to end on.
#
# It was 66 with the imaginarium in it. That room and the dog-leg up to it
# were twenty-two of those sixty-six and held nearly every blank frame in the
# film: seven seconds of a pale shaft climbed and then descended, and walks
# across floors that are plaster. Jake cut it on 2026-11-02, the same call and
# the same reason as the solarium. And 66 was already down from 90, which was
# a camera left running for half a minute after the clip had finished, because
# the number had not been looked at since the route was twice as long.
#
# It was 145 while the clip walked out to the solarium's sun, which is a
# hundred and nine metres from that room's threshold. Jake cut that on
# 2026-11-01 after watching a take: the walk out and back was a hundred and
# sixty of three hundred and forty-eight metres, almost all of it across a
# dark field, and the leg that crossed it was a lattice of sixty-seven
# thousand places that stopped the picture for twenty-five seconds. The clip
# stands at the way in and looks down the planet line now, which is the view
# that room is arranged for anyway.
CLIPS=$(cat <<'LIST'
rolling	2.5	9	5	hold:right:1.4 hold:up:1.2 hold:left:1.4 hold:down:1.2 o
stacking	4	13	7	shove:0.66:0.58:1.9 2.6 shove:0.26:0.43:1.9 2.6 k
cubes	0.5	10	6	1 l 2.5 l 2.5 l
teapot	1	7	4	hold:right:2.5 hold:q:1.5
teapot-glass	1	7	5	t 0.4 hold:right:2.5 hold:e:1.5
klein	1	7	4	hold:right:3 hold:q:1.5
klein-glass	1	7	5	m 0.3 t 0.4 hold:right:3
tunnel	0.5	7	4
sierpinski	1	7	5	1.5 up 2 up 2 down
menger	1	7	5	1.5 up 2.5 down
hilbert	1	7	5	1.5 up 2.5 down
ripple	1	9	5.5	1 space 1.6 space 1.6 space 1.6 space
chain	1.5	8	7.4	1 tap:right:20:0.035 3
knot	1	8	4	hold:right:1.6 m 1.2 m 1.0 down 1.2 up
load	3	15	6	1.5 m 1.5 up 1.5 up 1.5 up 2
arcade	2.5	46	16	film:clip
LIST
)

say() { printf '%s\n' "$*"; }

# Compile the table's keys into the log the engine plays. Spec 0084.
#
# The one place the grammar above is read. It used to be read here and then
# posted at the screen as CGEvents, with a table of macOS virtual key codes
# to go with it; the engine reads a log instead, and the names in the table
# are the names `KeyboardKey::named` already knows, so the table of codes is
# gone rather than moved.
#
# In awk because the times are fractions and a shell counts in whole numbers.
compile_keys() {
  local at=$1 wide=$2 tall=$3
  shift 3

  awk -v at="$at" -v wide="$wide" -v tall="$tall" '
    BEGIN {
      t = at + 0
      # How long a key that is tapped rather than held stays down. Two
      # frames of a sixtieth, so the frame between them has it down: both
      # events landing before one frame is a press the game never sees.
      tap = 2 / 60

      print "# compiled from the table in refresh-examples. Spec 0084."

      for (i = 1; i < ARGC; i++) {
        item = ARGV[i]
        n = split(item, part, ":")

        if (part[1] == "hold" || part[1] == "pin") {
          secs = part[n] + 0
          held = split(part[2], key, "+")
          # down in the order given and up in the other, which is how a
          # hand comes off two keys
          for (j = 1; j <= held; j++) printf "%.4f down %s\n", t, key[j]
          for (j = held; j >= 1; j--) printf "%.4f up %s\n", t + secs, key[j]
          t += secs
        } else if (part[1] == "tap") {
          many = part[3] + 0
          gap = part[4] + 0
          # a tap held longer than the gap to the next one would put its
          # up after the following down
          down = (gap > tap) ? tap : gap / 2
          for (j = 0; j < many; j++) {
            printf "%.4f down %s\n", t, part[2]
            printf "%.4f up %s\n", t + down, part[2]
            t += gap
          }
        } else if (part[1] == "shove") {
          printf "%.4f cursor %.1f %.1f\n", t, part[2] * wide, part[3] * tall
          printf "%.4f press left\n", t
          printf "%.4f release left\n", t + part[4]
          t += part[4] + 0
        } else if (part[1] == "zoom") {
          printf "%.4f cursor %.1f %.1f\n", t, wide / 2, tall / 2
          printf "%.4f wheel 0 %.4f\n", t, part[2] + 0
        } else if (item ~ /^[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?$/) {
          t += item + 0
        } else {
          printf "%.4f down %s\n", t, item
          printf "%.4f up %s\n", t + tap, item
          t += tap
        }
      }

      exit
    }
  ' "$@"
}

wanted=("$@")

say "== building the examples"
(cd blitzkit && cargo build --release --examples --quiet)
(cd arcade && cargo build --release --quiet)

# Where cargo put them, asked rather than assumed. The repos here share one
# target directory at the project root, so blitzkit/target does not exist and
# looking for the binaries there finds nothing.
TARGET=$(cd blitzkit && cargo metadata --format-version 1 --no-deps |
  python3 -c 'import json,sys; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["target_directory"])')/release
ARCADE=$TARGET/arcade

# media/ at the engine's root and not site/static/media, which is a copy
# site/scripts/build takes at build time and git ignores. Writing to the copy
# is writing to something the next build overwrites, which is where the first
# version of this put fourteen pictures.
OUT=blitzkit/media

# Logs go here and not into the repos: they are compiled from the table every
# run, so a committed one would be a second copy of the table that nobody
# reads.
LOGS=$(mktemp -d)
trap 'rm -rf "$LOGS"' EXIT

made=0
missed=0

while IFS=$'\t' read -r name settle seconds poster keys; do
  [ -n "$name" ] || continue

  if [ ${#wanted[@]} -gt 0 ]; then
    found=0
    for one in "${wanted[@]}"; do
      [ "$one" = "$name" ] && found=1
    done
    [ "$found" = 1 ] || continue
  fi

  say "== $name"

  tour=""
  case "${keys:-}" in
    film:*)
      tour="--film=${keys#film:}"
      keys=""
      ;;
  esac

  # The example to run, which is not always what the file is called: teapot
  # and teapot-glass are the same binary filmed in its two states, which is
  # what the page has always shown and what the readme still shows as two
  # stills.
  runs=${name%-glass}

  # and where it lives. The arcade is a binary in its own repo rather than an
  # example in the engine's, and it is on the site like the examples are, so
  # it is filmed like them.
  if [ "$runs" = arcade ]; then
    binary=$ARCADE
  else
    binary="$TARGET/examples/$runs"
  fi

  if [ ! -x "$binary" ]; then
    say "   there is no $binary to run"
    missed=$((missed + 1))
    continue
  fi

  # **Asked whether the route still works before anything is filmed.** Spec
  # 0042. The arcade's panel takes all four arrows while the sight is on it,
  # so a route that walks past the panel stops turning and keeps holding keys
  # at a camera that is pointed at a wall. That is what the clip published on
  # the 26th of October was, and no frame of it looks wrong.
  #
  # The game is asked rather than this file knowing the rule, because the rule
  # is the game's and a copy of it here would be a second account of which
  # arrows are yours.
  #
  # A planned route has no keys to check and is not checked here: what says it
  # goes where it says is `tour::tests::the_clip_s_tour_gets_all_the_way_round`,
  # which plans every leg of it in the gate, works the two doors on the way,
  # and names the legs that fail.
  if [ "$runs" = arcade ] && [ -n "${keys:-}" ] && ! "$binary" "--walk=$keys"; then
    say "   the staged route does not go where it says, so nothing was filmed"
    missed=$((missed + 1))
    continue
  fi

  # How big a frame is, asked of the thing that draws it. Raw pixels carry no
  # size, so ffmpeg has to be told one, and it has to be the engine's.
  read -r wide tall <<< "$("$binary" --film-size)"
  if [ -z "${tall:-}" ]; then
    say "   $name would not say how big its frames are"
    missed=$((missed + 1))
    continue
  fi

  log="$LOGS/$name.play"
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  compile_keys "$settle" "$wide" "$tall" ${keys:-} > "$log"

  # One run, drawn and encoded as it goes, through a fifo.
  #
  # **Not the run's stdout**, which was the first way and lasted one run. The
  # load example writes a row of comma separated costs per frame, so its film
  # came out with column headings in the middle of a frame and ffmpeg gave up
  # on it. A game is allowed to print. Down a fifo of its own, whatever it
  # says goes to /dev/null with the rest of its talk.
  #
  # -nostdin because the table this loop is reading comes in on this shell's
  # stdin and ffmpeg reads stdin for keyboard commands: it ate the rest of the
  # list once, and the run went from the first example straight to a line
  # beginning "7" and died on it.
  frames="$LOGS/$name.rgba"
  rm -f "$frames"
  mkfifo "$frames"

  ffmpeg -nostdin -hide_banner -loglevel error \
    -f rawvideo -pix_fmt rgba -s "${wide}x${tall}" -framerate 60 -i "$frames" \
    -r "$RATE" -c:v libx264 -preset slow -crf "$QUALITY" \
    -pix_fmt yuv420p -movflags +faststart -an "$OUT/$name.mp4" -y </dev/null &
  encoding=$!

  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  "$binary" $tour --play "$log" --film-to "$frames" --film-for "$seconds" \
    >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null || true

  if ! wait "$encoding"; then
    say "   nothing came out of it"
    rm -f "$frames"
    missed=$((missed + 1))
    continue
  fi
  rm -f "$frames"

  # and a still out of the moment this one is about, which is the poster the
  # clip shows before it loads and the picture a readme can use.
  #
  # Pulled out of the clip rather than drawn again.
  #
  # Drawing it again was the first way and is the better picture: no x264 and
  # no chroma subsampling between the engine and the file. It is wrong for one
  # of these and the one is enough. `--picture-to` runs the frames and draws
  # only the last, so a game that puts up what the last frame cost has never
  # had a frame to cost: load's readout came out as nought draws and nought
  # triangles at every second tried, where the clip of the same run has the
  # numbers. Spec 0084 says what that is and leaves it.
  #
  # The clip is the same bytes every time, so the frame taken out of it is
  # too.
  #
  # Written down per example rather than taken at some fraction of the length.
  # Six tenths of the way through chain is a floor covered in bricks with the
  # ball swung back up out of frame, which is a picture of the aftermath and
  # not of a wrecking ball. Not the first frame either, which for a thing
  # about to happen is a picture of nothing happening.
  #
  # Pick a second the picture holds across a whole swing rather than one that
  # catches an instant. chain's was 3.4, a few tenths before the ball struck,
  # and on 2026-10-28 a take ran the strike late and the still came out as a
  # wall nobody had touched. It is 7.4 now: the ball is down at the bottom of
  # its arc and the wall is in pieces, which stays true however the swing
  # drifts.
  #
  # The arcade's was 10, which on the route as it stands is the solarium: a
  # black field with one word on it, as the still that stands for a game
  # called arcade. It is 16 now, the walk down the hall, with cabinets either
  # side and three of the games named on them.
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  ffmpeg -nostdin -hide_banner -loglevel error -ss "${poster:-1}" \
    -i "$OUT/$name.mp4" -frames:v 1 "$OUT/$name.png" -y </dev/null

  # written down as it is filmed, so ./check-fresh can say how far behind this
  # clip is without anybody having to remember. The arcade has a repo of its
  # own on top of the engine and the rest do not, which is the whole of why
  # this takes two commits rather than one.
  if [ "$runs" = arcade ]; then
    ./stamp "$OUT/MADE" --from arcade "$name.mp4" "$name.png"
  else
    ./stamp "$OUT/MADE" "$name.mp4" "$name.png"
  fi

  say "   $OUT/$name.mp4 and $OUT/$name.png, $wide wide"
  made=$((made + 1))
done <<< "$CLIPS"

# And the sixteen as one picture, for a readme or a page that wants to show
# what is here before it says anything about it.
#
# Built from every poster on disc rather than from the ones this run touched,
# so a run over one example still leaves a grid that matches the rest. There
# is nothing to stamp for the same reason: a grid cannot be staler than its
# own tiles.
say "== the grid"
posters=()
while IFS=$'\t' read -r name _; do
  [ -n "$name" ] || continue
  [ -f "$OUT/$name.png" ] && posters+=("$OUT/$name.png")
done <<< "$CLIPS"

if [ ${#posters[@]} -eq 0 ]; then
  say "   no posters to lay out"
elif ! ./contact-sheet "$OUT/examples.png" "${posters[@]}"; then
  say "   the grid could not be laid out"
  missed=$((missed + 1))
else
  # Stamped against the engine like the posters it is made of, and with no
  # bakes: everything in it came out of this repo, so the one commit answers
  # for the grid and for every tile in it at once.
  ./stamp "$OUT/MADE" examples.png
fi

say ""
if [ "$missed" -gt 0 ]; then
  say "$made made, $missed missed."
else
  say "$made made."
fi
say "Watch them before committing: a staged clip can stage the wrong thing."
